EXT. SALT FLAT - TELEGRAPH STATION - NOON

White to the horizon. Heat shimmers. A sagging shack with a copper wire strung to nowhere.

ZEKE PIPKIN, 50s, telegraph operator, spectacles and sleeve garters, sits on the porch tapping a key that isn't connected to anything. He is humming.

Hoofbeats. Two riders emerge from the glare.

SILAS CRANE, 40s, long duster, silver at the temples, a rifle across his saddle. Beside him, MAHALA TWOFEATHERS, 30s, braided hair, wide-brimmed hat, a coiled rope at her hip. They slow to a walk and dismount without speaking.

ZEKE
Afternoon, folks. Wire's down.

SILAS
We know.

ZEKE
Been down since Tuesday.

MAHALA
We know that too.

Zeke stops tapping. Silas ties his horse to the porch rail with one hand, the rifle still in the other. Mahala watches the shack's window, then the roof, then the horizon, her fingers on the rope.

SILAS
Where's the payroll?

ZEKE
Payroll?

MAHALA
The Sunday coach. The strongbox. It was routed through here.

ZEKE
Nothing came through.

SILAS
Zeke.

ZEKE
Mr. Crane, I swear on my mother.

MAHALA
Your mother's dead.

ZEKE
That's why I swear on her.

Silas steps onto the porch. Boards groan. He peers through the window, then shoulders the door open.

SILAS
(inside)
Well.

MAHALA
What?

SILAS
(inside)
Come look.

She follows. Zeke trails after, wringing his hands.

INT. TELEGRAPH STATION - CONTINUOUS

Dim. A cot. A stove. A stack of crates labeled "PROPERTY OF THE WELLS CONSOLIDATED." In the middle of the floor: a heavy iron strongbox, lid open, empty.

SILAS
You said nothing came through.

ZEKE
Nothing came through. This came OUT.

MAHALA
Out of what?

ZEKE
Out of the floorboards.

Silas kneels. Pries up a plank. Beneath: a dark hole, a rope ladder dropping into earth.

SILAS
A tunnel.

MAHALA
To where?

ZEKE
I never looked.

SILAS
You never looked.

MAHALA
You live on top of it.

ZEKE
I never LOOKED!

Mahala drops to her knees. Lowers her head into the hole. A pause.

MAHALA
(muffled)
Footprints.

SILAS
Fresh?

MAHALA
Warm.

She pulls out, stands, and grabs the rope off her hip. Silas cocks the rifle. Zeke retreats toward the stove.

ZEKE
Now wait a minute. Wait. If you go down there and they're still down there, I'm the one who has to explain.

SILAS
Explain what?

ZEKE
Why I didn't wire it in.

MAHALA
There's no wire.

ZEKE
(small)
No. There isn't.

Silas peers into the hole again.

SILAS
Mahala. You take the rope. I'll go first.

MAHALA
No. I go first. You're too slow.

SILAS
I'm not slow.

MAHALA
You're thoughtful. It's a different kind of slow.

She swings her legs into the hole. Clambers down. Silas watches her disappear, then glances at Zeke.

SILAS
You stay.

ZEKE
I was going to.

SILAS
Don't touch the key.

ZEKE
It's not connected.

SILAS
Don't touch it anyway.

He lowers himself into the dark. The rope creaks. The rifle barrel scrapes the wood.

Zeke is alone. He drifts to the window. Looks at the white flat.

A line of dust far out on the salt. Riders. Many.

ZEKE (CONT'D)
(whisper)
Oh, no.

He shuts the door. Sets the bar. Presses his back to it, closes his eyes, and begins, softly, to hum.
